\hat{\rho}_{q} = Z^{-1} \sum_{k=1}^{N}\left|k\right\rangle_x\left\langle k\right|_x\,$$ where the summation $\sum_{k}$ runs over all elements of the ensemble, and $\left|k\right\rangle$ is the k’th eigenstate, $\langle k|$ its corresponding eigenvector. The quenched density operator has a direct physical interpretation in the thermal ensemble: it describes the system as it is found in the environment. In general, the density matrix of an arbitrary physical system describes correlations between system and environment as well as between the system itself. However, if the system is in contact with thermal equilibrium then the quenched density operator becomes the reduced density operator, which, strictly speaking, is defined in thermal equilibrium, see e.g. @xmath0 and references therein.

